then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc54df5c87f5dd569379a85e19d17b5db3774493db711a68a1e15485b9829a3b" "basis" 348 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-326014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc54df5c87f5dd569379a85e19d17b5db3774493db711a68a1e15485b9829a3b" "basis" 348 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-326014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e4e80d7502e27fd33982c3fb6f834c31333acc979c09703bef348ea980101a64" "basis" 348 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Cour